Z||000010||FIERO JOHN-WI||12-28-2001||08:19 AM||||Let's just say that FieroKyle is right that there are only 100,000 left, Isn't it sad that "We as a Fiero Community" can't get 300 Fieros together at any Fiero Event, Oh wait (the FOCOA Nationals in 1990 in INDY) THATS 11 SOON TO BE 12 YEARS AGO! So please do your part as a Fiero Owner and support ANY fiero event you can!  Just thought I would put in my 2 cents.  Plus Hope to see all of you at the 11th Annual Midwest Fiero Dells Run (just thought I would through in a quick plug while i'm on the subject) More info about the Dells Run 2002 will be posted in January, infact so much you'll have to attend just to find out, why everyone is talking about it!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fformula88||12-28-2001||12:01 PM||||Saw something about five years ago that put the estimate at about 250,000 still driveable.  I do not remember how they came up with that number, which did seem a little high.  Even taking it however, in the last five years I am sure a lot of Fiero's have been scrapped for either accident damage, or simply engines dying and the owners not wanting to pay for new motors.<P>I saw an 87 coupe in the junk yard last winter... the frame had next to no rust on it and the car looked nearly flawless except for faded paint.  It wasjunked because the engine died.  With the shape the frame was in it was a real shame to have seen it in the junk yard.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroKyle||12-28-2001||02:20 PM||||Alright evem if around 200,000 left. I figured it out and a Fiero dies every 49 minutes at that rate, so another 25 are destroyed a day. Its fascinating really.<P>Well if 7/10s of Fieros were destroyed and 360,000 were built, a little over 100,000 sounded right to me, but just a guess. ||||reg||1
